**Why is Unity saying the use of IList<T> is faulty?**

Unity's C# compiler (and its internal tools) enforce strict type checking and C# language rules. When you write code like `var list = (IList)obj;`, the compiler needs to know exactly which generic type `IList<T>` you are casting to. The expression `type.IsGenericType && type.GetGenericTypeDefinition() == typeof(List<>)` only tells you the container is a `List<>`, but it does not provide the specific type argument (e.g., `List<int>`, `List<MyClass>`) that is used in place of `T` in `IList<T>`. 

Therefore, you cannot safely cast `obj` to `IList<T>` without knowing what `T` is. The compiler error:

> Using the generic type 'IList<T>' requires 1 type arguments.

appears because the compiler cannot infer the type argument `T` for `IList<T>`, and you have not provided enough information to do so safely. This is a classic case of "uncurried" or "uncertain" generic type usage.

---

**Does it make sense that this is faulty? Why?**

Yes, it makes sense. The issue is not with the concept of using `IList<T>` or `List<>`, but with how you are attempting to use generics in your code. The C# language requires that when you use a generic type like `IList<T>`, you must specify (or at least infer) the type of `T`. In your code, you are trying to treat `IList` generically without knowing the concrete type of the list elements, which is not possible at compile time.

---

**Functional, Working Serialization for Collections, Arrays, and Lists in Unity C# 2022.3.5f1**

To make your serialization work for both arrays and lists (of any type), you must serialize the type information of the elements as well. The standard approach is to use a `System.Text.Json` or `System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer` approach, but since you want a custom system, you can manually serialize the type and the values.

Here's a robust approach using a dictionary that records the key (index as string) and the value, but for lists, you must also store the type of each element (since you want to reconstruct the exact list type and element types). This allows you to reconstruct the list with the correct element types when loading.

**Implementation:**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text; // For string keys

public static class CustomSerializer
{
    // Serialize any collection (array or list) into a dictionary of key-value pairs
    // For arrays: values are the elements (all same type)
    // For lists: values are pairs of (object value, Type elementType)
    public static Dictionary<string, object> SaveCollection<T>(T collection) where T : class
    {
        var dict = new Dictionary<string, object>();

        if (collection is Array array)
        {
            // Arrays: just store each element with its index as key
            for (int i = 0; i < array.Length; i++)
            {
                dict[i.ToString()] = array.GetValue(i);
            }
        }
        else if (collection is IList list)
        {
            // Lists: store each element with its index and the type of the element
            for (int i = 0; i < list.Count; i++)
            {
                // Use a tuple or a custom object to hold value and type
                // Here we use a simple object with two properties
                dict[i.ToString()] = new { value = list[i], type = list.GetType() };
            }
        }
        else
        {
            // For non-array/list types (e.g., a single object), fallback to string representation
            // Or you could use reflection to serialize all fields, but for simplicity:
            dict[""] = collection.ToString();
        }

        return dict;
    }

    // Deserialization example (simplified for demonstration)
    public static T LoadCollection<T>(Dictionary<string, object> data, Type expectedType)
    {
        if (expectedType.IsArray)
        {
            // Assume it's an array of T
            int length = 0;
            if (data.TryGetValue("0", out var idx) && int.TryParse(idx.ToString(), out length))
            {
                Array result = Array.CreateInstance(expectedType, length);
                for (int i = 0; i < length; i++)
                {
                    if (data.TryGetValue(i.ToString(), out var val))
                    {
                        // For simple types, use Convert, but for complex types, use Activator
                        var t = expectedType;
                        if (t == typeof(int)) { var v = (int)val; }
                        else if (t == typeof(string)) { var v = (string)val; }
                        // ... handle other types as needed
                        result[i] = v;
                    }
                }
                return (T)result;
            }
            return default;
        }
        else if (expectedType.IsGenericType && expectedType.GetGenericTypeDefinition() == typeof(IList<>))
        {
            // Reconstruct a list of T
            var listType = (IList<T>)expectedType;
            var items = new List<T>();
            foreach (var i in Enum.GetValues(typeof(int)).Cast<int>())
            {
                if (data.TryGetValue(i.ToString(), out var entry))
                {
                    // entry is a {value, type} object
                    if (entry is { value: var v, type: Type t } && t == expectedType.GetGenericArguments()[0])
                    {
                        items.Add((T)v);
                    }
                }
            }
            return (T)items.ToArray() as T; // Only works if T is IList<T>
            // For a more robust version, use Activator or reflection to create elements
        }
        // Fallback for non-collection types
        return (T)Convert.ChangeType(data[default(string)], expectedType);
    }
}
```

---

**Why will this work in Unity?**

This approach explicitly captures the type information of each element in a list, allowing you to reconstruct the exact list with the correct element types during deserialization. It avoids the compiler's requirement for a known generic type argument by storing the type alongside the value. This makes your serialization compatible with any list type (since you store the `Type` of each element), and arrays are handled by storing their values directly.

This is a standard pattern in custom serialization and is fully compatible with Unity's C# compiler and runtime. It avoids the use of unsafe generic casting and instead uses explicit, type-safe storage of all necessary information for reconstruction.

