accepted.answer:
Your scheduler is likely stuck in the `scheduler()` loop because of how you handle process preemption and context switching, especially in the round robin part for equal-priority processes.

**Key issues in your code:**

1. **No proper timer or time quantum handling for round robin:**  
   Round robin requires that each process gets a fixed time slice (quantum) before being switched out. In your code, you only switch processes when the currently running process is about to finish (i.e., you only context switch when `p->state == RUNNING` and you're looping through all processes with the same priority). This means if a process is running but not yet done, it will never be forcibly preempted and switched out, so it can run forever and block the scheduler.

2. **You don't track which process is currently running in the scheduler loop:**  
   The variable `c->proc` is set inside `swtch()`, but you don't update it correctly when you context switch. After a context switch, you set `c->proc = 0`, so on the next iteration, you'll always pick the same process (or the first one found), and the running process will never be properly tracked, leading to indefinite blocking or missed preemptions.

3. **Potential race condition with process state changes:**  
   When a process is running and you context switch to it, it must release its lock before being switched back. If it doesn't, you could deadlock or miss its state change. But more importantly, your round robin loop doesn't actually *time out* and switch to the next process in the ready queue after a fixed quantum.

---

## How to Fix It

### 1. Track the currently running process

Add a variable (e.g., `struct proc* current = 0;`) outside the loop to remember which process is currently running. When you context switch, update it.

### 2. Implement a time quantum for round robin

Decide on a quantum (e.g., 10 ticks). Each time a process runs, decrement the quantum. When it reaches zero, switch to the next ready process of the same priority (using a ready queue per priority).

### 3. Use a ready queue per priority

Instead of scanning all processes every time, maintain a list (or queue) of runnable processes per priority. This makes round robin and priority selection much easier and more efficient.

---

## Example Fix (Simplified Pseudocode)

Below is a sketch of how you might structure the scheduler with a ready queue per priority and proper quantum handling (adapt to your code style):

```c
// Add a ready queue for each priority (array of queues)
struct queue *readyqueues[21]; // assuming priorities 0..20

void scheduler(void) {
    struct proc *p;
    struct cpu *c = mycpu();
    struct proc *current = 0;
    int quantum = 10; // example quantum

    for (;;) {
        intr_on();

        // 1. Build ready queues by priority
        for (int pri = 0; pri <= 20; pri++) {
            readyqueues[pri] = NULL;
            for (p = proc; p < &proc[NPROC]; p++) {
                if (p->state == RUNNABLE && p->priority == pri) {
                    queue_add(&readyqueues[pri], p);
                }
            }
        }

        // 2. Find highest priority
        int max_prio = -1;
        struct proc *highproc = 0;
        for (int pri = 0; pri <= 20; pri++) {
            if (readyqueues[pri] != NULL) {
                // Pick the first in queue as representative
                struct proc *q = queue_get(&readyqueues[pri]);
                if (q->priority > max_prio) {
                    max_prio = q->priority;
                    highproc = q;
                }
            }
        }

        if (highproc == 0) {
            // No runnable processes
            continue;
        }

        // 3. Run the current process for a quantum
        struct proc *running = current;
        if (running != highproc) {
            // Switch to high priority process
            current = highproc;
            running->state = READY; // or whatever state is needed
            swtch(&c->context, &running->context);
            continue;
        }

        // Now, run current process for quantum
        int ticks = 0;
        while (ticks < quantum && running->state == RUNNING) {
            // Do one tick of work
            ticks++;
            // ... your process work here ...
            // If interrupted by an event (e.g., I/O), break and let scheduler continue
        }
        if (ticks == quantum) {
            // Time quantum expired: switch to next in ready queue of same priority
            struct queue *pq = readyqueues[running->priority];
            if (pq && pq->next) {
                struct proc *nextproc = pq->next->proc;
                current = nextproc;
                running->state = READY; // or whatever is needed
                swtch(&c->context, &running->context);
            } else {
                // No more in queue, go to next higher priority
                for (int pri = running->priority + 1; pri <= 20; pri++) {
                    if (readyqueues[pri] && readyqueues[pri] != NULL) {
                        current = queue_get(&readyqueues[pri]);
                        break;
                    }
                }
                if (current) {
                    running->state = READY;
                    swtch(&c->context, &running->context);
                }
            }
        } else {
            // Process finished early (e.g., I/O), put back to ready if needed
            current->state = READY; // or whatever state means ready
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Summary of Fixes

- **Track the currently running process.**
- **Use ready queues per priority for efficient selection and round robin.**
- **Enforce a time quantum so processes are preempted after a fixed interval.**
- **Properly update the running process after context switches.**

With these changes, your scheduler will no longer get stuck on a single process and will correctly implement round robin within each priority level.

