The below code takes an image `im` and degrades it into a degraded image called `y`:

im=imread(‘saturn.tif’);
x=fft2(im);
nn=1*1e+6;
x(10,10)=x(10,10)+nn; x(10,11)=x(10,11)+nn;
x(11,10)=x(11,10)+nn; x(11,11)=x(11,11)+nn;
y=real(ifft2(x));

How would I go about processing the degraded image `y` in MATLAB code, in order to achieve a restored image as close to the original image `im`

accepted.answer:
To restore the degraded image `y` back to the original `im`, you need to reverse the added noise (in this case, adding `nn` to specific FFT coefficients). Since the modification was to add `nn` to the FFT coefficients at positions (10,10), (10,11), (11,10), and (11,11), you should subtract `nn` from those same positions in the FFT before performing the inverse FFT.

Here is the MATLAB code to restore the image:

```matlab
y = real(ifft2(x)); % This is your degraded image (already computed)
X = fft2(y);         % Compute FFT of the degraded image

% Subtract the added noise from the specified FFT coefficients
X(10,10) = X(10,10) - nn;
X(10,11) = X(10,11) - nn;
X(11,10) = X(11,10) - nn;
X(11,11) = X(11,11) - nn;

im_restored = real(ifft2(X));
```

Assign `im_restored` to your restored image.

